SLEEP(3)                       P32W Unix                        SLEEP(3)

NAME
       sleep - suspend execution for an interval

SYNOPSIS
       #include <unistd.h>

       unsigned sleep(unsigned secs);

DESCRIPTION
       sleep suspends the process for secs seconds. It sets an
       alarm(2) for the time left and waits for it with pause(2),
       catching SIGALRM meanwhile; when the time is up it cancels
       the alarm and puts back the SIGALRM handler there was before.

       Other signals do not end the sleep: if one is caught, its
       handler runs, and sleep goes on waiting for the rest of the
       time. A signal that is not caught or ignored ends the process
       as usual.

       The clock counts whole seconds, so the sleep ends when the
       time of day reaches the second it started in plus secs: it
       lasts between secs - 1 and secs seconds. sleep(0) returns at
       once.

RETURN VALUE
       0.

EXAMPLES
       while (access("/tmp/lock", F_OK) == 0)
           sleep(1);

SEE ALSO
       sleep(1), alarm(2), pause(2), signal(2)

BUGS
       sleep uses the process's one alarm: an alarm set before the
       call is cancelled, not restored. There is no usleep or
       nanosleep; the finest step is a second (times(2) counts
       ticks of 1/50 s).
